guides.markdown:
@Validate attaches reactive field rules; trigger input/blur/submit deliberately. storeRules<T> checks a nested rule tree against your model; arrays and built-ins are whole-field rules rather than recursively guessed records. Cossack validateProperty/validateAll/getError/hasError/clearErrors expose UI error state. Custom async rules must account for stale edits and owner cleanup. validateValue/validateValueAsync/validateObject also work without a component.

Coercion validates a converted value; reactive field validation does not write that conversion back into state. validateObject returns coerced data. Required rules run before coercion, so empty strings do not become zero. Use InputSchema and @Server({input}) for typed untrusted payload boundaries; reactive Validate rules alone do not enforce server authorization or RPC input parsing. Internal metadata/rule registration helpers are not a replacement for decorators.

The portable example proves required rejection and explicit number coercion. Browser input triggers and error rendering require mounted components and generated browser acceptance.

